{"@type":"Process","@id":"820f4a3e-ee36-3206-9966-066bd496bf79","name":"Diesel combustion; turbine, cogeneration, industrial, SNCR; at turbine","description":"This unit process represents the combustion of diesel fuel in the specified equipment. The data was developed from National Energy and Technology Laboratory (NETL) datasets, which incorporate Webfire circa 2014 as the primry source of emissions factors. For emissions control technologies-- SCR refers to selective catalytic reduction, SNCR refers to selective non-catalytic reduction. Boilers that are labeled \"post-NSPS\" are referring to the U.S. EPA New Source Performance Standards.  \n","category":"Equipment","version":"01.01.000","lastChange":"2026-09-29T01:58:52Z","processType":"UNIT_PROCESS","defaultAllocationMethod":"PHYSICAL_ALLOCATION","isInfrastructureProcess":false,"location":{"@type":"Location","@id":"0b3b97fa-6688-3c56-88ee-4ae80ec0c3c2","name":"United States of America (the)","category":"Country"},"processDocumentation":{"validFrom":"2012-01-01","validUntil":"2022-12-31","timeDescription":"Data best represents 2012. Validity date range is based on an assumption of a 10 year equipment life span.","geographyDescription":"United States","technologyDescription":"Data represents combustion of diesel fuel in the specified equipment. Industrial refers to equipment used in manufacturing, processing, mining, and refining or any other industry to provide steam, hot water, and/or electricity.","inventoryMethodDescription":"Attributional","completenessDescription":"Relevant emissions to air captured, other flows such as ash or waste products from emissions control technologies are not included.","dataSelectionDescription":"NETL calculates combustion emissions based on the data reported in the EPA WebFIRE database. WebFIRE is the EPA\u0027s online database that contains emissions factors for criteria and hazardous air pollutants (HAP) for industrial and non-industrial processes and multiple reports submitted to the EPA using the Compliance and Emissions Data Reporting Interface (CEDRI) in response to regulatory requirements under Parts 60 and 63 of Title 40 of the U.S. Code of Federal Regulations (CFR).","dataTreatmentDescription":"NETL calculates combustion emissions based on the data reported in the EPA WebFIRE database. The database was filtered down to the intended fuel and equipment type, and emissions were normalized to the basis of 1 kg fuel combusted. Note, errors in the source dataset (e.g. unit conversion, typos, flow overlap) were identified and corrected.","samplingDescription":"For information on how data were collected, see Publication.","dataCollectionDescription":"Emissions data were collected from the EPA WebFIRE database.","sources":[{"@type":"Source","@id":"13381888-a763-33c7-aa84-8d0f23f1fc8b","name":"U.S. EPA (2012) WebFIRE"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"24aa88c8-dc43-360b-93ab-7ae3c9933bab","name":"State of New Jersey Department of Environmental Protection (2003) State of the Art (SOTA) Manual for Reciprocating Internal Combustion Engines\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"bc6fa442-1d15-3aa1-9c80-7580d01f1d08","name":"U.S. EPA (2007) Lists of potential control measures for PM2.5 and precursors, Draft Version 1.0\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"9e92c574-d722-38a7-a83b-7589ba87b99c","name":"NETL (2014) Combustion of Diesel"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"6a73f270-48ab-303f-852e-5ffe741854ff","name":"U.S. EIA (2014) Definitions of EIA Distillate Categories and Fuels Contained in the Distillate Grouping\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"9bc7782d-003a-3467-95e1-2b1051b4ea0f","name":"U.S. EPA (2015) Diesel Fuel"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"b39dcb37-92d0-352b-a17b-6fd92304b48e","name":"U.S. EPA (1999) Compilation of Air Pollutant Emission Factors, Volume 1: Stationary Point and Area Sources, Chapter 1: External Combustion Source, Section 3: Fuel Oil Combustion\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"67386e02-c945-31b7-823e-2d94cb45ef52","name":"U.S. EPA (2012) Report to Congress on Black Carbon"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"02c45b5c-2d25-385f-93f1-a819c0f1dc39","name":"NETL (2008) Development of baseline data and analysis of life cycle greenhouse gas emissions of petroleum-based fuels\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"ecee5491-88b8-3d9f-8491-5a2201077c67","name":"ERG (2026) NETL Stationary Combustion"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"2b4ab127-6b80-319e-9176-e71f8e11d788","name":"DieselNet (2008) Locomotives"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"e8fc0961-e9da-363e-b72e-ddfa80e415b5","name":"U.S. EPA (2010) Exhaust and Crankcase Emission Factors for Nonroad Engine Modeling - Compression-Ignition\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"a4492e56-acc0-3001-980a-bd11f9bb56fe","name":"U.S. EPA (2014) A-400 DRAFT Inventory of U.S. Greenhouse Gas Emissions and Sinks: 1990-2012\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"1221fe1c-8f2c-3be8-8ced-b458cc330ffe","name":"U.S. EPA (1994) Development and Selection of Ammonia Emission Factors"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"e529d250-f6a5-371c-a95a-eed6e41b04fa","name":"U.S. EPA (1999) Nitrogen Oxides (NOx), Why and How They Are Controlled\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"1c508427-9684-34bd-ac58-4ae41fdbf288","name":"U.S. EPA (2007) Diesel Generator Exhaust, SPECIATE 4.2"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"07c3713a-3942-3aee-89d9-c20a929a282c","name":"DieselNet (2013) Nonroad Diesel Engines"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"afceed81-032f-3950-b386-3f2adbe3b813","name":"U.S. EPA (2007) Diesel Exhaust, SPECIATE 4.2"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"533db7ef-a1ec-37d8-9d2b-3fb47808833c","name":"U.S. EPA (2008) Direct Emissions from Stationary Combustion Sources"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"50849744-3b38-3bab-90f3-067500f5bf21","name":"U.S. EPA (2005) Control of Emissions from New and In-Use Nonroad Compression-Ignition Engines: Emission Standards and Related Requirements, 40 C.F.R. §1039.101\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"6ade82f8-a341-302f-aa8c-b95aa0447434","name":"U.S. EPA (2001) Uncontrolled Emission Factor Listing for Criteria Air Pollutants\n"},{"@type":"Source","@id":"32d2f12b-887f-3bbd-aacc-25d31386be5